1
00:00:00,236 --> 00:00:01,036
Hi everyone

2
00:00:01,556 --> 00:00:02,533
This spring

3
00:00:02,601 --> 00:00:05,896
an e-commerce platform listed an AI interview assistant

4
00:00:06,265 --> 00:00:07,925
The product's official page said

5
00:00:08,282 --> 00:00:10,442
"Bestseller online, over 600,000 sold"

6
00:00:10,862 --> 00:00:12,782
"Hears questions live, just read it out"

7
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:15,893
But hiring companies are going the other way

8
00:00:16,177 --> 00:00:19,277
Google and McKinsey moved interviews back in person

9
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,877
and even require at least one in-person meeting before an offer

10
00:00:23,397 --> 00:00:26,549
In late October, a hiring manager at Anthropic posted

11
00:00:26,957 --> 00:00:29,117
that his team no longer gives coding tests

12
00:00:29,417 --> 00:00:30,457
They didn't get harder

13
00:00:30,761 --> 00:00:33,741
what changed is that models now do them far better than people

14
00:00:34,069 --> 00:00:36,929
After hundreds of practice problems, your first thought is

15
00:00:37,153 --> 00:00:39,015
So all these months, or even years

16
00:00:39,015 --> 00:00:42,193
of experience and problems I built up, was it all wasted

17
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:43,573
Don't panic yet

18
00:00:43,893 --> 00:00:46,313
The problems were always testing something else

19
00:00:46,413 --> 00:00:48,733
and that something just got cheap

20
00:00:49,111 --> 00:00:50,471
This is Pixel Breath

21
00:00:50,531 --> 00:00:52,089
Today let's talk about

22
00:00:52,205 --> 00:00:54,211
why grinding problems suddenly stopped working

23
00:00:56,536 --> 00:00:58,838
The interviewer poses a problem, you solve it live

24
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:00,298
they nod and figure you'll do

25
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:02,689
It looks like the test is measuring ability

26
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:06,809
but what the company wants to buy is how you'll work once you're in

27
00:01:06,929 --> 00:01:09,669
That's something no one can see in a one-hour interview

28
00:01:09,989 --> 00:01:12,527
If you can't see it, you need something else

29
00:01:13,147 --> 00:01:14,247
to show it

30
00:01:14,827 --> 00:01:18,604
Spence's 1973 paper is about exactly this stand-in

31
00:01:19,007 --> 00:01:20,189
His example: the diploma

32
00:01:20,538 --> 00:01:23,120
A diploma doesn't stand for your ability to do the job

33
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:24,740
But it works, so why is that

34
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:27,402
Because for the capable, it's cheap to get

35
00:01:27,642 --> 00:01:29,442
while for the less able, it's expensive

36
00:01:29,582 --> 00:01:31,540
and some can hardly get one at all

37
00:01:31,863 --> 00:01:35,345
Put simply, a signal works not because it resembles some ability

38
00:01:35,565 --> 00:01:37,605
but because faking it is expensive

39
00:01:37,956 --> 00:01:41,758
What a company sees in a diploma is how much you put into it

40
00:01:42,138 --> 00:01:43,596
Grinding problems is the same

41
00:01:44,076 --> 00:01:46,214
There was a student at Columbia University

42
00:01:46,321 --> 00:01:48,758
who spent 600 hours grinding to get into Big Tech

43
00:01:48,838 --> 00:01:51,456
about three months of full-time work

44
00:01:51,996 --> 00:01:55,056
What the interviewer sees in that hour is whether you're willing

45
00:01:55,322 --> 00:01:58,780
or able to put 600 hours into this job

46
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:02,135
What the problems always tested is how costly faking is

47
00:02:02,462 --> 00:02:04,642
So what if faking gets cheap

48
00:02:07,019 --> 00:02:08,523
That same student

49
00:02:08,584 --> 00:02:10,027
In March 2025

50
00:02:10,149 --> 00:02:10,982
at 21

51
00:02:11,310 --> 00:02:13,473
he used AI to build an app in under a week

52
00:02:13,573 --> 00:02:15,673
and ran it in the background during interviews

53
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:17,013
He said screen capture misses it

54
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:18,273
and so does the webcam

55
00:02:18,605 --> 00:02:20,297
The moment a question was asked

56
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,765
the answer would pop up right beside it

57
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:25,301
He used it to pass Amazon's interview

58
00:02:25,401 --> 00:02:28,114
and recorded the whole thing and posted it online

59
00:02:28,354 --> 00:02:31,534
Amazon and several other companies pulled their offers

60
00:02:31,794 --> 00:02:33,514
But the app kept going

61
00:02:33,849 --> 00:02:35,172
at $60 a month

62
00:02:35,311 --> 00:02:36,751
and plenty still bought it

63
00:02:37,112 --> 00:02:38,452
Around that same time

64
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:40,032
at an internal Google meeting

65
00:02:40,152 --> 00:02:43,131
an employee asked if in-person interviews could come back

66
00:02:43,532 --> 00:02:44,852
Pichai said on the spot

67
00:02:44,992 --> 00:02:47,909
worth thinking about, and some interviews went back in person

68
00:02:48,246 --> 00:02:49,629
It's the same in China

69
00:02:49,709 --> 00:02:51,369
This year, as banks hired new grads

70
00:02:51,584 --> 00:02:54,247
several added AI interviews to the process

71
00:02:54,607 --> 00:02:58,027
and AI interview assistants on e-commerce sites took off

72
00:02:58,367 --> 00:03:00,247
138 yuan for two hours

73
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:04,249
plans from 398 yuan up even correct your gaze

74
00:03:04,451 --> 00:03:06,174
so when you read answers in the test

75
00:03:06,234 --> 00:03:07,534
it won't show on the call

76
00:03:07,914 --> 00:03:10,414
At this point, the logic of the signal has changed

77
00:03:10,746 --> 00:03:13,414
That signal used to take 600 hours of effort

78
00:03:13,414 --> 00:03:14,769
before it showed results

79
00:03:15,108 --> 00:03:17,871
Now, with 138 yuan and two hours

80
00:03:17,931 --> 00:03:18,888
anyone can get it

81
00:03:19,348 --> 00:03:21,391
Economists call this pooling

82
00:03:21,731 --> 00:03:23,594
When everyone's signal is the same

83
00:03:23,694 --> 00:03:25,634
you can't tell who's who

84
00:03:26,054 --> 00:03:28,394
The way I see it, the problem is still there

85
00:03:28,494 --> 00:03:29,994
and so is the answer

86
00:03:30,212 --> 00:03:33,172
but it no longer lets companies see your ability

87
00:03:33,772 --> 00:03:34,975
Companies still look

88
00:03:35,155 --> 00:03:37,438
they've just switched to another signal

89
00:03:37,658 --> 00:03:40,818
McKinsey wants at least one in-person meeting before an offer

90
00:03:41,258 --> 00:03:43,498
Google has at least one in-person round too

91
00:03:43,853 --> 00:03:48,176
That Anthropic manager no longer gives coding tests

92
00:03:48,496 --> 00:03:52,056
he looks at weekend prototypes in resumes and portfolios

93
00:03:52,196 --> 00:03:55,733
for taste, and whether you've thought a feature through for users

94
00:03:56,098 --> 00:03:57,021
That same day

95
00:03:57,081 --> 00:03:59,981
Amazon posted its engineering guidelines on its website

96
00:04:00,321 --> 00:04:01,658
How many tokens you use

97
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:03,221
how much you use AI

98
00:04:03,565 --> 00:04:05,585
in Amazon's words, are these numbers reliable

99
00:04:06,205 --> 00:04:08,148
From now on, it's long-term business results

100
00:04:08,268 --> 00:04:10,788
This May, they also shut down an internal leaderboard

101
00:04:11,134 --> 00:04:13,914
because some people burned tokens like crazy to get on it

102
00:04:14,271 --> 00:04:15,914
The old signal was

103
00:04:15,994 --> 00:04:17,634
can you actually solve the problem

104
00:04:17,935 --> 00:04:19,558
The new signal is

105
00:04:19,678 --> 00:04:21,338
what have you actually produced

106
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:23,560
and it must survive follow-up questions

107
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:26,934
Did your software or product actually launch

108
00:04:27,034 --> 00:04:28,694
Who are its users right now

109
00:04:28,834 --> 00:04:30,351
Which part was yours

110
00:04:30,682 --> 00:04:33,645
The first one, AI can do for you overnight

111
00:04:33,867 --> 00:04:36,095
But the second, about products and users

112
00:04:36,095 --> 00:04:37,668
you have to answer yourself

113
00:04:40,087 --> 00:04:42,107
So what should we actually do now

114
00:04:42,607 --> 00:04:44,187
Let's first pin down the limits

115
00:04:44,827 --> 00:04:47,441
Whiteboards at in-person interviews still work

116
00:04:47,801 --> 00:04:48,867
faking is still costly

117
00:04:49,215 --> 00:04:51,309
And banks haven't dropped AI interviews

118
00:04:51,529 --> 00:04:53,869
instead, they added eye tracking and human review

119
00:04:54,249 --> 00:04:56,275
pushing the cost of faking back up

120
00:04:56,755 --> 00:04:58,269
What fails is the on-screen kind

121
00:04:58,589 --> 00:05:00,215
with standard answers to look up

122
00:05:00,557 --> 00:05:02,297
So here's a question to ask yourself

123
00:05:02,656 --> 00:05:04,150
That signal you've been building

124
00:05:04,310 --> 00:05:06,576
could someone else make it with AI overnight

125
00:05:07,056 --> 00:05:09,250
If they can, it's already worthless

126
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:12,356
If not, its price keeps going up

127
00:05:12,703 --> 00:05:14,051
In a Peking University class

128
00:05:14,313 --> 00:05:16,063
a student asked Professor Yang Jinfang

129
00:05:16,391 --> 00:05:18,325
An interview is only half an hour to an hour

130
00:05:18,525 --> 00:05:20,231
how can you really read a person

131
00:05:20,567 --> 00:05:22,021
The professor gave three filters

132
00:05:22,301 --> 00:05:23,427
each one a question

133
00:05:23,789 --> 00:05:25,383
What non-consensus view do you hold

134
00:05:25,623 --> 00:05:27,843
The professor also said it's normal not to know

135
00:05:28,123 --> 00:05:30,349
From my seat, I tried and couldn't answer either

136
00:05:30,849 --> 00:05:32,183
The second question follows

137
00:05:32,543 --> 00:05:34,649
How exactly did you reach that view

138
00:05:34,975 --> 00:05:37,915
You have to lay out the whole logic step by step

139
00:05:38,435 --> 00:05:40,695
This one, AI can make up for you too

140
00:05:41,275 --> 00:05:43,449
But once the interviewer presses a few times

141
00:05:43,629 --> 00:05:44,715
your answer falls apart

142
00:05:45,048 --> 00:05:46,442
Recruiters already see this

143
00:05:46,782 --> 00:05:49,602
There are reports of people scoring high in AI interviews

144
00:05:49,907 --> 00:05:52,361
then showing their gaps when grilled in person

145
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:55,225
Many HR people no longer trust those scores outright

146
00:05:55,445 --> 00:05:56,591
That's why it's expensive

147
00:05:57,091 --> 00:05:58,545
Back to the opening story

148
00:05:58,865 --> 00:05:59,965
The grind wasn't wasted

149
00:06:00,185 --> 00:06:03,404
look, what those 600 hours built is still in you

150
00:06:03,745 --> 00:06:06,965
It's just that companies won't verify it through a screen anymore

151
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:09,540
They want to see what you've really made

152
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:10,935
and what's at its core

153
00:06:11,251 --> 00:06:12,333
Back to that class

154
00:06:12,397 --> 00:06:13,925
a question I can't answer either

155
00:06:14,185 --> 00:06:16,171
What non-consensus view do I actually hold

156
00:06:16,731 --> 00:06:18,845
I won't answer it in this episode

157
00:06:19,085 --> 00:06:20,531
I'll tell you once I know
